   "evidence": "Lines 101-106 of the diff: the comment says '`erw` matches their definitionally equal subtype coercions'. Mathlib `coe_maxTrivEquiv_apply` and `toLinearMap_maxTrivLinearMapEquivLieModuleHom_symm` (Mathlib/Algebra/Lie/Abelian.lean:228,271) are stated for the Lie-submodule coercion, while `Submodule.coe_toBaseChange_tmul` is stated for the plain-submodule coercion.",
   "file": "TauCeti/Algebra/Lie/BaseChange/Intertwiners.lean",
   "fix": "Prove a short auxiliary lemma giving the coercion of the composite `toBaseChange` and `LinearEquiv.ofEq` applied to `a \u2297\u209c x`. Or state the `ofEq` step between the Lie submodules' `toSubmodule`s so the coercions match syntactically. Then close with `rw`/`simp only` instead of `erw`.",
   "issue": "`toLinearMap_baseChangeEquiv_tmul` ends with `erw [LinearEquiv.coe_ofEq_apply, Submodule.toBaseChange.toLinearEquiv_apply, Submodule.coe_toBaseChange_tmul, toLinearMap_maxTrivLinearMapEquivLieModuleHom_symm]`. This relies on the coercion from the Lie submodule `maxTrivSubmodule` and the coercion from the ordinary submodule `Submodule.baseChange` being definitionally equal. The comment admits it, but it is still the brittle cross-wrapper defeq pattern, and `baseChangeEquiv_tmul` is built on it.",
   "line": 105
  }
